  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Saturday...\n\nAn upper low sliding east through southern Canada is working to\nflatten the upper ridge over the region today, with the ridge \naxis shifted east into the Dakotas. A weak frontal boundary is\npushing through the area, turning winds out of the northwest and\nbringing in some drier air. High temperatures will top out in the\n90s to near 100 degrees today, warmest over the east. The \ncombination of some weak jet energy, falling heights and the\nfrontal passage will allow isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms to develop over the southern mountains and foothill \nareas this afternoon. Convection will spread east into the early\nevening, lingering into the overnight over the far southeast. SPC\nhas a marginal risk for severe weather this afternoon and evening\nfor areas south and east of Billings. While CAPE (500 to 1000 \nJ/kg) and deep layer shear (20 to 30 kts) is modest at best. There\nis potential for a few stronger thunderstorms. Heavy rainfall, \nstrong winds and hail are all threats with the thunderstorms.\n\nMonday looks to be mostly dry, as monsoonal moisture is pushed \nsouth of the region. A backdoor cold front associated with the\naforementioned low sliding east through southern Canada will push\nthrough the area during the day bringing cooler temperatures\n(upper 80s to lower 90s) and breezy north to northeast winds,\nstrongest near the Dakotas border. Smoke from wildfires across the\nPac NW and southern BC will move through the area bringing some\nvisibility reductions at times. \n\nEnsembles remain in good agreement heading towards the middle of \nthe week in a return of ridging to the region, as well as an \nincrease in low level and monsoonal moisture. This will keep \nmoderate chances for daily showers and thunderstorms in the \nforecast, greatest over the mountains and foothills as weak\nimpulses and shortwaves move through the region. Currently, \nWednesday and Thursday have the greatest chance for thunderstorms.\nNear to slightly above normal temperatures are forecast for most \nof the week with highs in the upper 80s to 90s most days. Heading \ninto next weekend, there is potential for hotter conditions, with \nmore widespread 100 degree temperatures. STP",
